# Go source is stored with LF, whatever the checkout does locally.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, so the working tree
# has CRLF and `gofmt -l` lists most files — noise that is purely local, since the
# committed bytes are LF and gofmt-clean. That is fine until someone with
# autocrlf=false commits, at which point CRLF enters the history and the noise
# stops being local: gofmt would then be right, and the diff would be every line
# of the file.
#
# Declaring it here makes the stored form a property of the repo rather than of
# whoever happened to clone it. Nothing is renormalised by adding this — the
# committed bytes already match.
*.go text eol=lf

# The shell scripts are executed by CI on Linux and macOS. A CRLF shebang line
# fails with a bare "bad interpreter", which is a confusing way to learn about
# line endings.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell files are deliberately NOT declared here. They are stored LF today,
# and both `-text` and `eol=crlf` would rewrite all five of them: the first tells
# git to store the working tree's CRLF verbatim, the second asks for CRLF in the
# index. Leaving them unmanaged keeps the stored bytes as they are. Checked with
# `git add --renormalize .`, which staged 664 changed lines across five .ps1
# files before this line was removed and none after.
